\paragraph{Complementary constraint roles.}
ESS dominance explains why $\bstar$ is stable across $\rho$ (\S\ref{sec:robustness}): the majority of batches are governed by a constraint that depends only on within-batch weight concentration, not population contamination rate.
The $\aeff$ constraint, though binding in a minority of batches (${\sim}$37\%), is critical for effectiveness: it compresses $\bstar$ to ${\sim}$20\% of the ESS upper bound in those batches (median 0.80 vs.\ 3.59), preventing the over-intervention that makes ESS-only reweighting no better than uniform (Table~\ref{tab:main}).
The two constraints thus serve complementary roles---ESS provides proportion-robust baseline behavior, $\aeff$ provides targeted quality intervention where needed---and neither alone suffices.

