\subsection{Higher Contamination}
\label{sec:robustness}

To test sensitivity to contamination severity, we evaluate at $\rho = 0.6$ (seed\,42 only).
Adaptive $\bstar$ (PPL 17.740) maintains its absolute advantage over uniform (17.766), but the margin narrows from 0.041 at $\rho{=}0.4$ to 0.026, and the gap to grid-best ($b{=}0.5$, PPL 17.704) widens from 0.005 to 0.036.
Comparing degradation across contamination levels, adaptive exhibits the largest PPL increase ($+$0.087), followed by uniform ($+$0.072), grid $b{=}0.5$ ($+$0.053), and ESS-only ($+$0.040).
The fixed exponent $b{=}0.5$ degrades least among effective methods, suggesting that $\tau{=}0.8$ may be too aggressive when contamination rises.
A $\tau$ sweep at $\rho{=}0.6$ confirms this: $\tau{=}0.7$ achieves the best PPL (17.730), slightly outperforming $\tau{=}0.8$ (17.740).
Across tested contamination levels, $\tau \in [0.7, 0.8]$ is optimal (Figure~\ref{fig:tau_rho_rank}); the mild rank reversal motivates a per-$\rho$ $\tau$ schedule (e.g., $\tau \approx 1 - \rho/2$) that could partially recover the gap.

ESS-only (PPL 17.770) falls within 0.004 of uniform (17.766); its small PPL increase reflects its low baseline effectiveness at $\rho{=}0.4$ rather than contamination resilience---a method that provides no benefit cannot lose much when conditions worsen.
With more synthetic samples per batch, the ESS floor binds earlier, preventing the solver from raising $b$ enough to suppress them effectively.

These are single-seed results and should be interpreted accordingly.

